# tail-toto — Project Overview

## What This Project Is

`tail-toto` is a faithful CLI replica of the GNU Core Utilities `tail` command.
It prints the **last part** of each input (by default the last 10 lines) to
stdout, then exits. Input is each `FILE` operand in order, or **stdin** when no
operand is given or the operand is `-`. It does not follow files (`-f` is out of
scope), so it never loops waiting for new data.

Versions: **v1.0.0** (git tag) is the POSIX-style subset (plain decimal NUM,
no suffixes, exact long options, `--h` / `--v` shortcuts).

**v2.0.0** (current) is GNU-compatible: it matches GNU coreutils `tail` 9.4
for every invocation that does not need follow mode.

Input strategy (chosen at **run time** per input):

| Input kind | Mechanism | Heap |
|------------|-----------|------|
| Seekable regular file | `lseek()` from the end, read backwards in fixed chunks | none |
| Non-seekable (pipe, terminal, stdin redirect from pipe) | stream forward, keep last N lines/bytes in a buffer | yes, freed before return |

Platform I/O (chosen at **build time** by the Makefile):

| Build | Mechanism |
|-------|-----------|
| POSIX | `open` / `read` / `lseek` / `write` |
| Windows (`TAIL_TOTO_WIN32_IO`) | same calls + `O_BINARY` and `_setmode(..., _O_BINARY)` on stdin/stdout |

## Binary Name

The compiled binary must always be named **`tail-toto`**. Never rename it.
It is produced at **`build/tail-toto`** (MinGW may also emit
`build/tail-toto.exe`; that is the same program). The unit-test binary is
**`build/tests/test_core`**.

## Platforms

| Platform | Build environment | Notes |
|----------|-------------------|-------|
| **Linux / WSL** | `gcc`/`clang` + GNU Make | POSIX I/O; full unit tests |
| **Windows** | **MSYS2 UCRT64** or **Git Bash** + UCRT64 `gcc` on `PATH` | Makefile auto-selects `TAIL_TOTO_WIN32_IO` when the `<io.h>` / `_setmode` probe succeeds |
| **Git Bash** | Put `/c/msys64/ucrt64/bin` **before** other toolchains on `PATH` | Avoid minimal MinGW toolchains; see `toolchain-structure.mdc` |

Binaries are **not portable** across operating systems — build separately on Linux and on Windows.

## Scope — Keep It Minimal

Supporting behaviour (all as in GNU `tail` 9.4):

- `--help` / `--version`, processed **in order** with the other options: the
  first one reached wins, and an earlier usage error is reported instead
- `-n NUM`, `-nNUM`, `--lines=NUM`, `--lines NUM` — last `NUM` lines (default 10)
- `-n +NUM` — output starting with line `NUM` (1-based; `+0` behaves as `+1`)
- `-c NUM`, `-cNUM`, `--bytes=NUM`, `--bytes NUM` — last `NUM` bytes
- `-c +NUM` — output starting with byte `NUM`
- A leading `-` on NUM is ignored; a leading `+` is **sticky**: once seen, it
  stays in effect for later `-n` / `-c` options (`-n +3 -n 2` = from line 2)
- NUM: optional leading whitespace, optional `+`, decimal digits, optional
  multiplier suffix: `b` 512, `kB` 1000, `K`/`k` 1024, `MB` 1000², `M`/`m` 1024²,
  then `G`, `T`, `P`, `E`, `Z`, `Y`, `R`, `Q`; `xB` = power of 1000, `xiB` = power
  of 1024; a suffix alone means 1 (`K` = 1024). Overflow → "Value too large" error
- Obsolete syntax `-NUM[bcl]` / `+NUM[bcl]` when it is the only option: alone,
  with one FILE, or followed by `--` and at most one FILE (`-5`, `+5`, `-3c`,
  `-2b`, `-l`, `+`). Digits anywhere else → `option used in invalid context -- 5`
- `-q`, `--quiet`, `--silent` — never print `==> name <==` headers
- `-v`, `--verbose` — always print headers
- `-z`, `--zero-terminated` — line delimiter is NUL, not newline
- Follow-only options, with GNU's behaviour when not following: `--retry`
  (warning), `--pid=PID` (validated; warning when PID ≠ 0), `-s N` /
  `--sleep-interval=N` (validated, ignored), `--max-unchanged-stats=N`
  (validated, ignored)
- Long options may be abbreviated to any unique prefix (`--lin`, `--h`);
  `--v` is ambiguous (`--verbose` vs `--version`), `--s` is ambiguous
  (`--silent` vs `--sleep-interval`)
- Short options may be clustered (`-qv`, `-qn5`); `-n` / `-c` / `-s` consume the
  rest of the token or the next argument
- Options and FILE operands may be interleaved (`tail-toto a.txt -n 3`), unless
  `POSIXLY_CORRECT` is set (then the first FILE ends option parsing)
- `--` ends option parsing; `-` as an operand means stdin
- Multiple files → headers by default; continue past unreadable files
- CLI flag strings live in `tail_toto_cli.h` (`TAIL_TOTO_ARG_*`); the option
  parser compares against those macros only

**Do not add:**

- `-f` / `-F` / `--follow` (follow mode)
- GNU's hidden `---presume-input-pipe` option
- Interactive or TUI modes
- Logging or debug output in normal operation
- Single-dash `-h` as help (`-h` is an invalid option, as in GNU `tail`;
  `-v` is **verbose**)
- Any feature that would break GNU `tail` behavioural compatibility for the
  supported options

**Remaining differences from GNU `tail`** (deliberate):

- No follow mode: `-f` / `-F` / `--follow` are rejected (GNU ignores `-f` on a
  pipe); long-option prefixes of `--follow` (`--f`, `--fo`) are unrecognized
- The obsolete forms with `f` (`-5f`, `+5f`) are not recognized
- `_POSIX2_VERSION` is ignored (behaves as GNU's default, POSIX 2008)
- Program name `tail-toto`; `--version` prints `tail-toto <version>` only;
  `--help` text is tail-toto's own
- Write failures are always reported as `write error: <reason>` (GNU reports
  failures of large outputs as `error writing 'standard output'`)
- `<reason>` texts come from the platform C library (they differ on Windows)

## Behavioural Contract

| Invocation | Output | Exit |
|---|---|---|
| `tail-toto file` | last 10 lines of `file` | `0` |
| `tail-toto -n 3 file` | last 3 lines | `0` |
| `tail-toto -n +3 file` | from line 3 to end | `0` |
| `tail-toto -c 5 file` | last 5 bytes | `0` |
| `tail-toto -c 1K file` | last 1024 bytes | `0` |
| `tail-toto -n 0 file` / `-c 0` | nothing; no FILE is opened, so no headers and no open errors | `0` |
| `tail-toto -5 file` / `+5 file` | last 5 lines / from line 5 (obsolete syntax) | `0` |
| `tail-toto -z -n 2 file` | last 2 NUL-terminated records | `0` |
| `cmd \| tail-toto` | last 10 lines of stdin | `0` |
| `tail-toto a b` | `==> a <==\n…\n\n==> b <==\n…` | `0` |
| `tail-toto -q a b` | contents only, no headers | `0` |
| `tail-toto -v a` | `==> a <==\n…` | `0` |
| `tail-toto --retry file` | stderr: `tail-toto: warning: --retry ignored; --retry is useful only when following`; last 10 lines | `0` |
| `tail-toto --pid=1 file` | stderr: `tail-toto: warning: PID ignored; --pid=PID is useful only when following`; last 10 lines | `0` |
| `tail-toto --version` / `--vers` | prints `tail-toto <version>\n` | `0` |
| `tail-toto --help` / `--h` | prints usage on stdout | `0` |
| `tail-toto missing` | stderr: `tail-toto: cannot open 'missing' for reading: <reason>` | `1` |
| `tail-toto missing ok` | error for `missing`, `ok` still printed | `1` |
| `tail-toto -n abc f` | stderr: `tail-toto: invalid number of lines: 'abc'` | `1` |
| `tail-toto -c abc f` | stderr: `tail-toto: invalid number of bytes: 'abc'` | `1` |
| `tail-toto -n 99999999999999999999 f` | stderr: `tail-toto: invalid number of lines: '99999999999999999999': Value too large for defined data type` | `1` |
| `tail-toto -99999999999999999999` | stderr: `tail-toto: invalid number: '-99999999999999999999': Numerical result out of range` | `1` |
| `tail-toto -n 2 -3` | stderr: `tail-toto: option used in invalid context -- 3` | `1` |
| `tail-toto -s abc` | stderr: `tail-toto: invalid number of seconds: 'abc'` | `1` |
| `tail-toto --pid=abc` | stderr: `tail-toto: invalid PID: 'abc'` | `1` |
| `tail-toto --max-unchanged-stats=abc` | stderr: `tail-toto: invalid maximum number of unchanged stats between opens: 'abc'` | `1` |
| `tail-toto -n` | stderr: `tail-toto: option requires an argument -- 'n'` + hint | `1` |
| `tail-toto --lines` | stderr: `tail-toto: option '--lines' requires an argument` + hint | `1` |
| `tail-toto -x` | stderr: `tail-toto: invalid option -- 'x'` + hint | `1` |
| `tail-toto --foo` | stderr: `tail-toto: unrecognized option '--foo'` + hint | `1` |
| `tail-toto --v` | stderr: `tail-toto: option '--v' is ambiguous; possibilities: '--verbose' '--version'` + hint | `1` |
| `tail-toto --quiet=x` | stderr: `tail-toto: option '--quiet' doesn't allow an argument` + hint | `1` |
| Read failure | stderr: `tail-toto: error reading '<name>': <reason>` | `1` |
| Write failure | stderr: `tail-toto: write error: <reason>` (stops immediately) | `1` |

Options are processed left to right: `--version --help` prints the version,
`-x --help` reports the invalid option. Usage errors are detected before any
input is read or output is written. Stdin is named `standard input` in headers
and diagnostics. A final line without a trailing delimiter still counts as a
line and is printed as-is.

## Exit Codes

| Code | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `0` | Success — every input processed (or `--help` / `--version`) |
| `1` | Error — usage error, any unreadable input, or write failure |

